= Module 3: Human Geography Through the Corridors of Time

== 3.1 The Historical Trajectory of Disciplinary Paradigms
Human Geography evolved across distinct historical epochs in response to geopolitical and intellectual revolutions (NCERT Table 1.1).

#figure(
  image("assets/geog_hum_evolution_timeline.png"),
  caption: [Evolutionary Sequence of Paradigms from Early Colonial Exploration to Post-Modernism],
)

== 3.2 Deep Analysis of the Six Historical Periods

1. *Early Colonial Period (Exploration & Description):*
   - Motivated by imperial trade discovery (Age of Discovery). Explorers mapped uncharted coasts and compiled encyclopedic inventories of flora, fauna, and mineral wealth.
2. *Later Colonial Period (Regional Analysis):*
   - Elaborate regional monographs were compiled based on the holistic philosophy that every region is an organic component of the planetary whole. This knowledge aided colonial administration and resource extraction.
3. *1930s through Inter-War Period (Areal Differentiation):*
   - Championed by *Richard Hartshorne*. Emphasized the unique personality of particular regions (*idiographic approach*), investigating why and how one geographic area differed from another.
4. *Late 1950s to Late 1960s (Spatial Organisation & Quantitative Revolution):*
   - Replaced qualitative regionalism with computer modeling, statistical laws, and geometric physics analogues (Christaller, Von Thünen). Human behavior was modeled on the rational *Economic Man* (*Homo economicus*).
5. *1970s Radical Backlash (Welfare, Radical, and Behavioural Schools):*
   - Disillusionment with sterile mathematical formulas that failed to address war, poverty, and urban ghettos led to three revolutionary schools focused on human well-being, Marxian analysis, and cognitive perception.
6. *1990s to Present (Post-Modernism in Geography):*
   - Rejection of all-encompassing universal theories ("meta-narratives"). Focus on localized narratives, cultural relativity, plural voices, and the deconstruction of spatial power structures.

